================================================================================
  CROSS-n SYNTHESIS: THE RECURSIVE PATTERN
================================================================================

  BLUE FRACTION BY REGION:
    n    overlap     bottom        top       apex    overall
    4        N/A      0.000      0.000      0.333      0.143
    5      1.000      0.724      0.724      0.571      0.744
    6      0.660      0.652      0.652      0.708      0.660

  OVERLAP CELL FRACTION (approaches 1 as n grows):
    n= 4: C(1,2)/C(3,2) = 0/3 = 0.0000
    n= 5: C(2,2)/C(4,2) = 1/6 = 0.1667
    n= 6: C(3,2)/C(5,2) = 3/10 = 0.3000
    n= 7: C(4,2)/C(6,2) = 6/15 = 0.4000
    n= 8: C(5,2)/C(7,2) = 10/21 = 0.4762
    n=10: C(7,2)/C(9,2) = 21/36 = 0.5833
    n=15: C(12,2)/C(14,2) = 66/91 = 0.7253
    n=20: C(17,2)/C(19,2) = 136/171 = 0.7953
    n=50: C(47,2)/C(49,2) = 1081/1176 = 0.9192

  THE FORMULA:
    blue_fraction(n) ~ overlap_fraction + (1-overlap_fraction) * wiring_blue
    ~ C(n-3,2)/C(n-1,2) + (2(n-3)+1)/C(n-1,2) * wiring_blue
    -> 1 as n -> infinity (because C(n-3,2)/C(n-1,2) -> 1)

  WHY OVERLAP IS BLUEST:
    Flipping a tile in the OVERLAP changes the inner tournament on n-2 vertices
    while keeping BOTH endpoint wirings fixed. The SC anti-automorphism,
    if it existed, maps the inner tournament to its complement while swapping
    the wirings. Changing ONLY the inner part tends to preserve this structure
    because the wiring remains compatible.

    The APEX is the least blue because flipping it directly changes whether
    the tournament has a Hamiltonian cycle through the base path, which
    strongly affects the SC structure.

  RECURSIVE META-GRAPH FORMULA:
    G_n/Z_2 is built from two copies of G_{n-1}/Z_2 sharing G_{n-2}/Z_2,
    plus one apex bit. The edges decompose as:
    E(n) = E_overlap(n) + E_wiring(n) + E_apex(n)
    where E_overlap lifts edges from G_{n-2}/Z_2 into G_n/Z_2,
    E_wiring adds edges from endpoint insertion choices,
    and E_apex adds edges from the cycle-controlling bit.

  DONE.
